# fxmind — auto Task

For implementation requests read `.fxmind/modes/task.md`; new resource → `.fxmind/modes/create.md`; large rewrite of an existing resource → `.fxmind/modes/refactor.md`. Questions/reviews remain read-only unless changes are requested. "Analyze and fix" authorizes implementation; respect decisions already made and user stop.

For repository questions AND implementation, start discovery with the FxMind MCP: call `fxmind_query` once for relevant indexed memory and confirm claims in current source. If memory has no coverage or you need symbols/locations, use `fxmind_search` in the affected directory. Native Grep/ripgrep is a fallback after the FxMind lookup, not the default first step; direct reading of known files is fine. If FxMind MCP is unavailable, disclose that and use native search. Use MCP for gates and available domain operations. Missing MCP blocks gated edits, not read-only diagnosis.

Quick/full controls effort, not quality. Trivial means a known tiny one-file change with no new behavior; UI and risky tasks use A/B. Gates: start → A → B → implement + checks → V with actual evidence → C. Gate V automatically dispatches a fresh independent reviewer for every task, using relevant skills and a strong model from the same provider when available. If V returns findings, fix them without asking the user, rerun affected checks and retry V; only strict VERIFIED passes. Keep sessionId; claim task files for parallel edits. Never write gate/session JSON.

Read `.fxmind/modes/task-verify.md` before UI edits and before V. UI requires browser interactions, visual inspection and an existing screenshot/trace. Build alone cannot verify UI. Failed/blocked checks leave V incomplete, and edits after V require re-verification.

FiveM: `.fxmind/policy/fivem-principles.md` is binding. Keep constants/helpers/files only when they add concrete value. Match local conventions; preserve security checks. Record gate notes once instead of duplicating ceremony in chat. User-facing replies are short and direct — outcome first, no long text.
